# Attention

Mechanism: people notice what maps to their current task, stack, or pain. Novelty helps only when also category-appropriate. Source credibility matters most here — the reader can't yet evaluate claims — and decays fast once they can.

Do:
- Lead with the reader's task, stack, or pain; make relevance instant.
- Spend credibility signals here: recognizable logos, named experts, standards compliance. They are wasted deeper in the funnel.
- Invest design effort in the first-seen surface (thumbnail, hero, first frame): visual-appeal judgments form in ~50ms and persist. Clean and fluent, not lavish.

Don't:
- Lead with the technology ("revolutionary AI-powered…").
- Make novelty claims with no task anchor.
- Rely on credibility badges to substitute for substance later in the funnel.

Example:
- ✗ "Revolutionary AI-powered code intelligence for modern teams."
- ✓ "Cut review time on large PRs: Acme flags the three files that matter, inside your GitHub flow."
